<!--
  CLion CMake profiles for this ESP-IDF project.

  IMPORTANT: building on the host is NOT supported. All builds run inside the
  ESP-IDF dev container (ESP-IDF v6.1). Each subproject's CMakeLists.txt
  enforces this by requiring DOCKER_IMAGE=esp32_sensors, which is set by the
  Dockerfile.

  CLion reaches the container through the Dev Container defined in
  .devcontainer/devcontainer.json, which reuses the `dev` service from
  docker-compose.yml. CMake, Ninja, and the Xtensa/Linux toolchains therefore
  run in the container.

  FILE FORMAT
  ===========
  CLion stores these profiles through CMakeSharedSettingsStorage, whose XML
  state is the element *children* of <component name="CMakeSharedSettings">.
  It reads `configurations` -> `configuration` and understands only these
  attributes:
      PROFILE_NAME, ENABLED, CONFIG_NAME, TOOLCHAIN_NAME,
      GENERATION_DIR, BUILD_OPTIONS, GENERATION_OPTIONS,
      GENERATION_PASS_SYSTEM_ENVIRONMENT, ADDITIONAL_GENERATION_ENVIRONMENT,
      NO_GENERATOR, FROM_PRESET

  Do NOT use the pre-2023 shape (`<option name="configurations"><list>` with
  BUILD_DIR / CMAKE_OPTIONS / GENERATOR_NAME): the current loader finds no
  `configurations` child there, so the project ends up with ZERO CMake
  profiles and the CMake run configuration fails with "cannot find any
  profile". Edit the profile in Settings | Build, Execution, Deployment |
  CMake, or update the attributes above - never reintroduce the old names.
  In particular a profile has NO "generation directory" of the *source*
  project - it has no way to point CMake at a subdirectory. See the KNOWN
  LIMITATION at the end of this comment for what that means in practice: the
  profiles are meant to be one per subproject, but today every one of them
  configures the repo root instead.

  ONE PROFILE PER SUBPROJECT (code model / refactoring)
  ======================================================
  The repo root is NOT an ESP-IDF project: its CMakeLists.txt is a plain
  aggregator, `project(... NONE)`, that only defines custom targets which
  shell out to `idf.py`. A profile aimed at the root therefore enables no
  C/C++ languages, produces no compile_commands.json and gives CLion nothing
  to index - no navigation, no refactorings.

  Each subproject, on the other hand, is a standalone ESP-IDF project and
  ESP-IDF turns on CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS by default
  (esp-idf/tools/cmake/project.cmake), so configuring any of them emits a
  compile_commands.json that CLion turns into a full resolve configuration.
  Hence four profiles, keyed by profile name to the matching CMake run
  configuration in .idea/runConfigurations/:

      profile                       subproject          CLion build dir
      Espressif ESP32-S3            mcgowan/            cmake-build-esp32s3
      Host tests (test)             test/               cmake-build-linux-test
      Host tests (espidf-cpp-test)  espidf-cpp-test/    cmake-build-linux-espidf-cpp-test
      Linux app (donny)             donny/              cmake-build-linux-donny

  The root aggregator (`scripts/dev.py build`, or the "esp32_sensors
  (container CMake)" run configuration) remains the way to build *everything*
  in one shot; the per-subproject profiles exist for the IDE's code model.

  BUILD DIRECTORIES
  =================
  Each profile keeps its OWN build tree, named cmake-build-*, so CLion never
  shares a CMake cache with the canonical `idf.py` trees that
  `scripts/dev.py` writes (mcgowan/build_esp32s3, test/build_linux,
  espidf-cpp-test/build_linux, donny/build_linux). Sharing a directory does
  not work: idf.py and CLion overwrite each other's CMake cache and Ninja
  state. All cmake-build-* directories are git-ignored (`.gitignore`).

  COEXISTING RESOLVE CONFIGURATIONS
  =================================
  The four projects share sources - mcgowan/src is compiled by both mcgowan
  and test, and components/* by three or four of them - and mcgowan (Xtensa,
  esp32s3) and the others (host, linux) genuinely need different defines and
  include paths, notably which of hal_board / hal_stub is in view. CLion
  keeps each profile's resolve configuration separate and switches with the
  active profile (the run configuration's CONFIG_NAME), which is why every
  profile must be kept, not merged.

  TOOLCHAIN_NAME is the Dev Container toolchain ("Default", the system
  toolset inside the container); the generator is left unspecified so CLion
  uses Ninja. GENERATION_OPTIONS also pins -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug so the
  compile database carries debug info and CLion's default CONFIG_NAME=Debug
  matches a configuration that actually exists.

  KNOWN LIMITATION: the profiles above do NOT actually configure their
  subproject yet, so the code model they were meant to provide does not
  exist. GENERATION_DIR is only the *build* directory; the *source* directory
  comes from the single CMake workspace, .idea/misc.xml
  (CMakeWorkspace PROJECT_DIR="$PROJECT_DIR$"), which is the repo root for
  every profile. Observed after a clean CLion rebuild: all four
  cmake-build-* trees record CMAKE_HOME_DIRECTORY=/project, none contains a
  compile_commands.json, each one's codemodel names project
  `esp32_sensors_subprojects`, and the only targets are the four aggregate
  custom targets (donny_build, espidf-cpp-test_build, mcgowan_build,
  test_build) - all of type UTILITY, i.e. no compilable translation units. A
  profile has no per-profile source directory, so fixing this means opening
  the subprojects as separate CLion projects, or giving CLion a merged
  compile_commands.json for the root. Until then, navigation and refactorings
  are not driven by these profiles; `scripts/dev.py build` remains the way to
  build everything.
-->
<project version="4">
  <component name="CMakeSharedSettings">
    <configurations>
      <configuration PROFILE_NAME="Espressif ESP32-S3" ENABLED="true" CONFIG_NAME="Debug" TOOLCHAIN_NAME="Default" GENERATION_DIR="$PROJECT_DIR$/mcgowan/cmake-build-esp32s3" GENERATION_OPTIONS="-DIDF_TARGET=esp32s3 -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug" />
      <configuration PROFILE_NAME="Host tests (test)" ENABLED="true" CONFIG_NAME="Debug" TOOLCHAIN_NAME="Default" GENERATION_DIR="$PROJECT_DIR$/test/cmake-build-linux-test" GENERATION_OPTIONS="-DIDF_TARGET=linux -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug" />
      <configuration PROFILE_NAME="Host tests (espidf-cpp-test)" ENABLED="true" CONFIG_NAME="Debug" TOOLCHAIN_NAME="Default" GENERATION_DIR="$PROJECT_DIR$/espidf-cpp-test/cmake-build-linux-espidf-cpp-test" GENERATION_OPTIONS="-DIDF_TARGET=linux -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug" />
      <configuration PROFILE_NAME="Linux app (donny)" ENABLED="true" CONFIG_NAME="Debug" TOOLCHAIN_NAME="Default" GENERATION_DIR="$PROJECT_DIR$/donny/cmake-build-linux-donny" GENERATION_OPTIONS="-DIDF_TARGET=linux -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug" />
    </configurations>
  </component>
</project>
